<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salar Jung Museum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1951</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Salar Jung Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> houses the collection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salar-jung-iii">Salar Jung III</a>, including paintings, sculpture, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, arms, textiles and decorative arts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. It opened in 1951 and was declared a museum of national importance in 1961. It is among the largest museums in India.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salar-jung-museum.jpg" alt="Salar Jung Museum" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Omer123hussain · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salar_Jung_Museum,_Hyderabad,_India.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
